Final status model (user, 2026-10-25): specs carry `alignment` (unreviewed | aligned | misaligned; editing a spec resets it to unreviewed). Plans, briefs AND todo lists share ONE lifecycle: proposed → approved → completed, approved → proposed when revised, proposed|approved → abandoned. completed and abandoned are final, and `in-flight` was retired. An approved plan means its implementation is in flight. A brief is approved with its plan and completes after it. QA's todo list needs approval before the fix loop runs. Building needs an approved plan (and brief); fixing needs an approved todo list. The user approved the plans: 9 plans in build order frontmatter → scaffold → install → cli/init → buildVersionsMeta → cleanup → discover → validate → src, plus the shared src/tools/frontmatter/brief.aide. Bootstrap exception: aide_frontmatter doesn't exist yet, so the architect set `status: approved` by hand, once, on the 9 plans and the brief. The build runs one implementor per numbered step. A new guard test, src/retiredVocabulary.test.ts, is red by design until the main agent's prose sweep lands; no release before that.

