## The architectural intent (what the specs say after the rewrite)

1. **Required sections.** New brain.aide sections are REQUIRED, not optional. A missing section fails boot with a specific message naming it.
2. **Contract source.** The required-section list is the contract, published only from brainContract. The template is only the default fill. A custom-backend config that satisfies the contract is valid.
3. **Repair scope.** Repair inserts only the missing sections, taken from the LATEST bundled template. Existing sections, wiring and customizations are never touched.
4. Superseded by #21.
5. **Fifth status.** It is `brain-aide-invalid`, with a tagged fault:
   - missing-sections, which names the missing sections;
   - malformed, which gives the reason.

   A missing file stays `no-brain-aide`. An unwired slot stays `no-mcp-entry`. A present file is never reported as "not present".
6. Superseded by #20.
7. Superseded by #13.
8. **One `kanban` skill** replaces the 7 kb-* skills.
   - It is a thin pointer that does not depend on AIDE, so it works in non-AIDE projects.
   - The same file is shipped by AIDE and copied verbatim as the user's global skill.
9. **No `feature` field.** Task ↔ feature linking is out of scope (separate kanban task).
10. **Existing bug fixes included:**
    - aide_init's brain category was gated on a stale brainPath argument (removed).
    - The no-mcp-entry wiring flow got "run sync" instead of the config prose; the brain.md rewrite now reads the section from the file.
11. **Unreadable brain.aide.** A present-but-unreadable brain.aide (any read error but ENOENT) reports `brain-aide-invalid` with a malformed fault carrying the read error, e.g. "cannot read .aide/config/brain.aide: path is a directory (EISDIR)".
    - The parser has a sixth parse kind, `unreadable`. The status vocabulary stays five.
    - Upgrade maps unreadable to malformed, never missing.
    - Sync refuses with the reason.
12. **Single-source guard scope.** The single-source guard (src/service/parseBrainAide/singleSource.test.ts) scans src/ string literals AND every .md/.aide under .claude/ and .aide/docs/ for real contract marker tokens.
    - Ephemeral pipeline files are excluded.
    - /aide:brain config derives the config section's markers from brainContract.
13. **The template is Obsidian-specific and the kanban guide is split (user, 2026-10-08).**
    - **The bundled Obsidian template's section text is written for Obsidian:** wikilinks, the Kanban plugin's board format and trailing `%% kanban:settings` block, `kanban-plugin` frontmatter, and the real brain tool names.
    - **It reproduces the user's working kanban exactly**, with only references to the user's own projects excluded. Its source is the user's customized brain copy.
    - **The guide is split into a hub plus one file per operation.**
      - Seed sections: `kanban-hub` → `kanban/kanban.md`, plus `kanban-create` / `-work` / `-manage` / `-move` / `-complete` / `-stash` / `-review` → `kanban/guide/<op>.md`. The architect may refine these names.
      - The `work-kanban` section is removed.
      - The contract goes from 8 to 15 sections.
    - **The kanban skill** stays backend-neutral and points to the hub, then the operation file.
    - **Repair on a non-template backend** adapts both live and seed sections.
14. **Launch modes (user, 2026-10-09).**
    - Setup asks global or local, global recommended.
    - Global = bare command name. Local = pinned with `-y`, never `@latest`.
    - A missing global package gets a consent-gated install, with fallback to pinned local.
    - The choice sticks.
    - Upgrade judges npx / aidemd-mcp / custom; custom entries are left alone.
    - /aide:upgrade moves the aide pin; /aide:brain config moves the brain pin.
    - aide_info has a separate `behind` notice.
    - No user-facing @latest anywhere, enforced by a guard test.
    - publish.mjs updates the README pins.
15. **Board tracking lives on the AIDE side (user, 2026-10-09).** The /aide boot sequence checks the project's open tasks through the kanban skill and cross-checks them against session.aide; the orchestrator keeps the feature's task current through kanban skill operations (start → In Progress, stash at transitions, complete at close), never silently. session.aide carries a required `## Kanban` section linking every task tied to the feature by slug. Tasks still carry no feature field (#9 stands); the dependency stays one-way, AIDE → kanban.
16. **Board work goes through the `kanban-maintainer` agent (user, 2026-10-09).** The board is too large for the main context. The agent reads the board and tasks and reports what work is available (title, excerpt, path per task); the main agent uses that report to ask the user what to work on or continue the session's task, may read any single task by its path, and redelegates every change back to the agent by slug. The agent never runs approval gates — the main agent holds them. It ships with the kanban skill as a pair (AIDE-independent), installed by aide_init.
17. **No board file; per-project task folders (user, 2026-10-09).** The user no longer uses the Obsidian kanban plugin, and the single board (456 lines, ~99 KB, ~10 projects per column) is unreadable for agents. Folders are the board: kanban/<project>/<column>/<slug>.md (backlog, todo, in-progress, in-review, done); a task's folder is its column; task frontmatter carries project, priority, optional type, created, updated. No plugin frontmatter, settings block, checkboxes or wiki-link board lines. This supersedes #13's plugin board format for the kanban seeds (the kanban-board seed is dropped; the index and guides are rewritten); the rest of #13 stands. It must land before publish. Migrated 2026-10-09: 667 tasks; folder column won over board column; board.md and backups in kanban/legacy/.
18. **session.aide is never deleted (user, 2026-10-09).** It is the long-lived session record, linked to the board. aide_cleanup never reads, writes or deletes it. The maintainer, /aide Completion, handoff skill and session-aide.md now say so.
19. **Strategy is triggered by the pipeline test (user, 2026-10-08).** Strategy needed = several modules each doing one thing in a specific order, handing off through calls, events or queues; independent of Research needed. Parent spec carries the end-to-end flow, each module its slice; self-contained modules stay frontmatter-only. Decisions parked in session.aide or brief.aide never justify skipping.
20. **Content-free brain responses (user, 2026-10-10).** aide_init's summary brain step carries only `brainSummary` (flags, markers, sizes) on every status — the heavy fields are removed from the type. Missing sections are inserted tool-side by aide_upgrade category=brain (byte-identical only when the launcher is the template's mcpvault integration; otherwise named for the user to adapt), matching the file's line endings, returning only inserted markers. One migration per round trip: aide_upgrade brain with `brainChange: <marker|"launcher">` and no `beforeHash` is a read-only preview returning one before/after + `beforeHash` (lowercase hex SHA-256 of the exact before string); the same call with `beforeHash` applies, refusing on any byte change. aide_brain accepts any contract marker and returns one section verbatim on every status where the file exists and parses; its reply is exactly status, instructions, found (`found` true only when instructions is the section's bytes). Malformed remediation gives the parser's reason, a hand fix, and a re-check; never "run /aide:brain config". No explicit consent flag; no line numbers.
21. **Brain config authoring is its own skill (user, 2026-10-10).** Validation stays structural — every required field and section must exist; content is the user's responsibility. Authoring a brain.aide for any backend becomes a standalone skill (task `brain-authoring-skill-create-a-brain-aide-for-any-backend`); /aide:brain config keeps detection, Obsidian repair, migration, validation and sync, and hands create and custom-backend adapting to that skill once it exists.
22. **session.aide is always current (user, 2026-10-10).** The orchestrator updates session.aide through aide-handoff whenever anything changes what it says — not only at pipeline milestones — and never ends a turn or a session with it behind.
23. **session.aide is the full session record, linked to the board (user, 2026-10-10).** It holds what was done, every decision settled, what is validated and what comes next, in full — a fresh agent needs only this file to resume. `## Kanban` slug links stay required. Task stashes continue alongside but never replace the record. This reverses the 2026-10-09 'index to the board' slim-down (c913d8d).
24. **Versions manifest hygiene (user, 2026-10-10).** One plan at src/tools/upgrade/buildVersionsMeta covers:
    - `fetch-depth: 0` on the checkout in publish.yml and ci.yml (ci.yml runs `npm run build`).
    - A new src helper `assertFullClone(repoRoot)`. It runs `git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository`, resolves only on exactly "false", and throws on "true" or anything else. It is wired into scripts/build-versions.ts after the git --version check and exits 1 like the existing aborts.
    - package.json gets `"clean": "node -e \"require('node:fs').rmSync('dist',{recursive:true,force:true})\""`. `build` starts with `npm run clean &&`, `prepublishOnly` becomes `npm run build`, and `files` gets `"!.aide/versions.json"`.
    - `git rm .aide/versions.json` plus a .gitignore entry.

    No change to parseGitLog, previousCommit, readVersionsManifest or resolveOutdated. The first full-history publish changes every artifact's sourceCommit vs v0.10.4, so hosts see everything outdated once more on that upgrade. This is a one-time correction.
25. **Brain authoring skill is `aide-brain-author` (user, 2026-10-10).** It is a canonical shipped skill. Its SKILL.md is prose and is edited directly by the main agent. Its registration in the install, upgrade and versions-manifest code goes through the pipeline.
26. **aide_brain parameter shape (architect, user-approved and built; 2026-10-10).** `aide_brain { source?, kind?, path? }`:
    - `source` is one of "host" | "template" | "contract" | "draft". Omitted means "host".
    - Host (default): kind is any contract marker, omitted means "orientation". It returns `{ status, instructions, found, purpose? }`, unchanged.
    - template: kind is any contract marker or "launcher", omitted means "orientation". It returns `{ template, purpose }` with the bundled template's verbatim bytes. The launcher is the frontmatter region in the default local launch mode. The launcher purpose lives in brainContract (launcherPurpose), not in the contract array.
    - contract: takes no other param. It returns `{ sections: [{ marker, role, purpose }] }` in contract order, with no text and no launcher.
    - draft: `path` is required and project-relative. It returns `{ outcome: valid | invalid (fault missing-sections/sections or malformed/reason) | absent | refused (outside project; nothing read, path not echoed), instructions }`. It uses parseBrainAideFromString and classifyParseFailure, so faults match the host check.
    - BrainInput is a zod union of strict variants. Rejected: launcher outside template, kind with contract or draft, path outside draft, unknown keys.
    - No mode writes to disk. Draft remediation never points to aide_upgrade or /aide:brain config.
27. **aide-brain-author skill shape (orchestrator prose, 2026-10-10).**
    - The skill writes a whole config to `.aide/config/brain.aide.draft`, checks it with `aide_brain({ source: "draft" })`, then: swaps it in when no config existed; on replace, renames the current file to `.aide/config/brain.aide.bak` first; on beside, leaves the draft.
    - Its repair mode (adding missing sections, handed off from /aide:brain config's adapt branch) edits the live config in place, then re-checks with aide_info.
    - It asks the launch mode itself with the same Global/Local wording as /aide:brain config.
    - It never runs sync or seeds the brain; /aide:brain config owns wiring and seeding.
28. **aide_cleanup closure rule (user, 2026-10-11).**
    - Where a plan.aide exists, the plan must be fully checked, and any todo.aide present must be fully checked. A missing todo never blocks. allowEmptyPlan lets only a plan with no checkboxes pass.
    - Where there is no plan.aide, a fully checked todo.aide is removed on its own. Refused: a brief.aide (removed only together with its plan); a todo.aide with no checkboxes; a folder with no ephemerals. allowEmptyPlan is ignored there.
    - A refusal names each unchecked item by file and line and removes nothing.
29. **session.aide always exists, and comes from aide_scaffold (user, 2026-10-25).** It is an empty placeholder (intent 'Nothing recorded yet.' plus every canonical section heading, each empty) from aide_scaffold type "session", the single template source. aide_init instructs the agent to call it, and /aide boot calls it when the file is missing. Every handoff is an UPDATE that writes whatever the agent has. It never refuses for missing content, and ## Kanban may be empty. aide_scaffold never overwrites, so nothing resets a filled record. This amends #15 and #23; board tracking still stands but never gates a write.
30. **aide_scaffold never overwrites; replacement is a separate delete (user, 2026-10-25).** Every type (intent, research, both, todo, plan, session) is a no-op on an existing target, reported as such. Replacing a file means removing it through aide_cleanup first.
31. **Abandon mark (user, 2026-10-25).** plan.aide, todo.aide and brief.aide can carry the `abandoned` status, set through aide_frontmatter by an agent only after the user says to discard the work (for example, abandoned halfway through to start over, or a never-used blank scaffold). aide_cleanup removes a file that is fully checked or marked abandoned. The rule is per file (an abandoned plan next to an open todo list is refused), and an abandoned plan takes its brief. Cleanup never sets or infers the mark. allowEmptyPlan is removed. session.aide is never removed. This amends #28.
32. **Recorded state through aide_frontmatter (user, 2026-10-25).** Two governed fields. Specs carry `alignment` (unreviewed/aligned/misaligned; editing a spec resets it to unreviewed; the old `status: aligned|misaligned` on a spec is read as alignment and renamed on the next write). Plans, briefs and todo lists share one `status` lifecycle (proposed/approved/completed/abandoned; approved drops to proposed when revised); `in-flight` is retired. aide_frontmatter enforces the transitions (no skipped steps; completed and abandoned are final; completed only with every item checked; a brief completes only with its plan), never removes a value, changes only the one line, and refuses the session record, research specs, non-AIDE files and outside paths. It never decides when a value changes. Who approved a plan goes in session.aide, not in a status. No `revising` or `review-bypassed` status. This repo's specs get a one-time backfill.
33. **Build gate (user, 2026-10-25).** Building needs the plan, and its brief if present, approved; fixing needs the todo list approved. /aide:build, /aide:fix and the aide-implementor each read the status through aide_frontmatter's read mode first (exact line `<path> — status: approved`) and stop otherwise. Revising an approved file drops it back to proposed.
34. **Spec edits reset alignment (user, 2026-10-25).** The spec writer and strategist set alignment back to unreviewed through aide_frontmatter after editing a spec; the aligner restores aligned. The backfill sets install and scaffold (rewritten today) to unreviewed, and cleanup and frontmatter (no value) to unreviewed; all others keep their value under `alignment`.
35. **aide-upgrader agent (user, 2026-10-25).** A new canonical agent, shaped like kanban-maintainer. It never talks to the user. REPORT mode finds every AIDE file on an older format: intent specs on the old `status` name or with no alignment, and plans/todos/briefs with no status. For each one it suggests a value with evidence from the checkboxes, the git history of what the steps built, a todo beside it, and session/task mentions. APPLY mode carries out exactly the changes the user approved, through aide_frontmatter only, and verifies each one. The upgrader runs at the end of EVERY /aide:upgrade, reading the changelog's [migration] entries; there is no notice and no condition. Specs migrate as one batch on a single yes; each status-less plan/todo/brief is confirmed with the user, using the upgrader's suggestion. Future format changes reuse the same agent. Code: register the new agent wherever installs/upgrades list agents, plus the versions manifest (pipeline). Prose: the agent definition, and /aide:upgrade's final step (main agent).
36. **The fix loop is autonomous (user, 2026-10-25).** QA's todo list is agents checking agents. The orchestrator triages it, approves it itself through aide_frontmatter, and routes each item by misalignment tag: implementation-drift/test-gap → implementor; plan-gap/strategy-gap → architect amends the plan first; spec-gap/spec-bloat → spec-writer/strategist; research-gap → domain expert. The user is interrupted only for an outcome change, a scope change, or an item still failing after two attempts; otherwise they hear one line in the completion report. Out-of-scope defects stay unchecked with a note and are offered as tasks. Written into the aide skill's Stage 7 and /aide:fix.
37. **Changelog drives migrations (user, 2026-10-25).** CHANGELOG.md at the repo root (Keep a Changelog) ships to hosts as .aide/docs/changelog.md. Format-changing bullets start with [migration] and carry exactly Detect / Migrate / Confirm (Confirm: once, as a batch | per file). aide-upgrader reads only those bullets, detects by the file's current form (idempotent, catches up skipped releases), and uses the host's git history as evidence for per-file suggestions. This repo's CLAUDE.md requires an [Unreleased] entry with every user-visible change and a [migration] entry for every format change; a release missing one is not shippable.
38. **Session record from aide_scaffold only (user, 2026-10-25).** Boot, aide-handoff and the spec writer's CREATE all call aide_scaffold type=session; nobody writes the placeholder by hand. aide_init's session step only instructs the agent to call it; the CLI calls the same scaffold function.

## Anti-regression invariants future build phases must hold

- The section list and marker text exist ONLY in brainContract. A test guards against restatement.
- No backend-specific tool names or link syntax (vault, obsidian, note(s), wikilink, hub(s), [[, mcp__, kanban-plugin) in the contract purposes, the kanban skill and agent, or shipped docs/prose outside the template. Template section bytes are exempt.
- The kanban skill and guide never reference aide_brain, .aide/, or the orientation section.
- The status vocabulary stays closed at five values, and every consumer handles all five.
- No real contract marker token (aide-<marker>-start/-end) may appear in .claude/ or .aide/docs/ prose; the guard test enforces it.
- No user-facing `@aidemd-mcp/server@latest` in src/ (guard test), .claude/, README.md or .aide/docs/.
- Changes to existing code are surgical (doctrine in plan-aide.md and the architect, implementor and QA agents).
- **session.aide always exists and never blocks a write.** Every task tied to the work is linked by slug when there is one. A handoff is never refused or held back for missing content or a missing task. The install seeds the placeholder once and never overwrites the file. Shipped aide/handoff/spec-writer prose stays backend-neutral (no storage vocabulary).
- **aide_cleanup touches only plan.aide, todo.aide, brief.aide in the named module** — fixed names, never a directory listing, never session.aide, specs or code.
- **Pipeline ephemerals are removed only through aide_cleanup** — never a shell delete; the maintainer verifies the retro, then calls the tool; dryRun is never a required step.
- **A template section edit appends the previous fingerprint** — when any bundled brain template section's prose changes, append its old fingerprint to the outdated-sections record and update the drift pin, or old configs stop being recognized.
- **No tool returns more brain text than one decision needs** — the aide_init summary brain step stays under 4 KB on every status (test); aide_brain returns one section; aide_upgrade's brain preview returns one before/after.
- **Check `git status` before publishing** — publish.mjs commits everything already staged.
- **Published manifests come from a full clone** — scripts/build-versions.ts must refuse to build on a shallow clone; every workflow that builds keeps `fetch-depth: 0`; this repo never commits or ships `.aide/versions.json`.
- **Template read and contract listing never read the host config** — their answers are identical whatever state .aide/config/brain.aide is in; draft validation reuses the host check's parser and fault mapping and refuses paths outside the project.
- **A completed, abandoned or fully checked no-status ephemeral is never stranded** — aide_cleanup removes it (completed re-checked) with or without a plan beside it; anything still proposed or approved is refused; a brief.aide never goes without its plan.
- **aide_scaffold never overwrites any existing file** — every type is a no-op on an existing target.
- **No build or fix starts on anything not approved** — building needs the plan (and its brief, if present) approved, and fixing needs the todo list approved, each checked through aide_frontmatter's read mode by the build/fix stage and the implementor.
- **Recorded state changes only through aide_frontmatter** — never a hand edit of alignment or status.

